00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.400
So what we want to
do here is we want

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.580
to use a live stream
of telemetry data

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.170
that is coming from taxis
in the New York City area,

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.210
and we want to build a series
of data paths for this data.

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:21.960
We're going to process
it, and we're going

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:23.850
to power different experiences.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:28.290
One of those experiences
could be a live visualization

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:34.770
of this data as it is occurring
on the New York Metro area.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.860
As you can imagine we're going
to be relying on Cloud Pub/Sub

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:40.260
to deliver these
messages, and it probably

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:43.530
wouldn't be a wise choice to
bill this application directly

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.240
in the client.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:48.110
Wouldn't be a bad choice
because your client,

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:50.070
if you have a single
machine that's

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:52.170
in charge of
displaying this data

00:02:52.170 --> 00:02:53.545
and doing all
these computations,

00:02:53.545 --> 00:02:55.586
you're going to end up up
with an experience that

00:02:55.586 --> 00:02:56.610
looks a lot like this.

00:02:56.610 --> 00:03:00.960
Basically, an ever-growing
queue of undelivered messages.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:05.840
That's probably not what
you want in a lively map,

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:07.680
or in a lively display.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:09.792
You will continue to
process this data,

00:03:09.792 --> 00:03:11.250
but the results
that you're getting

00:03:11.250 --> 00:03:13.720
are not lively-- you're
actually falling behind.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:16.920
So this suggests that we
need, for this portion

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.910
of our processing, a
different strategy.

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:24.760
There are also likely to be
other requirements in addition

00:03:24.760 --> 00:03:26.620
to liveliness,
because you're dealing

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:30.110
with data that, at the end
of the day, results in money.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:34.210
So correctness, completeness,
reliability, scalability,

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:36.640
and performance are
kind of built in when

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:38.860
you process your data here.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.020
We need to honor those.

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:44.110
We also have this
requirement of liveliness

00:03:44.110 --> 00:03:46.780
which suggests continuous
event processing,

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:48.850
and another thing that
we're going to be doing

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:52.390
is we're going to be archiving
the data as it comes along,

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.240
with some transformations,
for further use.

00:03:55.240 --> 00:04:00.040
Since we have systems that
allow us to store data

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:02.602
at a very good
economical rate we

00:04:02.602 --> 00:04:04.060
can just be archiving
everything we

00:04:04.060 --> 00:04:07.510
see for analysts to
later study and mine

00:04:07.510 --> 00:04:10.750
and derive some more insights,
in addition to the ones

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.210
that you are doing live.

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:17.040
Another thing that's common
in this type of activities

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:20.224
is being mindful of your
Application cc Management--

00:04:20.224 --> 00:04:22.515
how do you coordinate updates,
how do you patch things.

00:04:22.515 --> 00:04:26.640
So we're going to be talking a
little bit about that as well.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:28.920
Let's start with one
concrete example.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:30.150
Let's count right.

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.189
So Slava, take it away.

00:04:32.189 --> 00:04:33.480
SLAVA CHERNYAK: Thanks, Rafael.

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:38.260
So let's talk about what we'd
like to do with our taxi data.

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.410
Our taxi telemetry is
arriving and it's already

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:42.784
said that we'd like to condense
it into something that we

00:04:42.784 --> 00:04:45.577
can process on a single
machine in our visualizer.

00:04:45.577 --> 00:04:47.160
We would like to use
Dataflow to write

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:49.380
a Pipeline to do this for us.

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:52.260
time That would take raw taxi
telemetry and transform it

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:54.217
into something condensed
in time and space,

00:04:54.217 --> 00:04:55.800
but we'd like to do
this in a way that

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:58.110
preserves enough information
for our visualization

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:00.550
to still be accurate.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:03.500
We're going to step through
and talk about how we do this.

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:06.310
So the raw taxi telemetry data
would look something like this.

00:05:06.310 --> 00:05:08.680
You know, we have latitude
and longitude points,

00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.636
we have time stamps about the
most recent location of a taxi,

00:05:11.636 --> 00:05:13.510
and we'll have some
other additional metadata

00:05:13.510 --> 00:05:16.690
such as how many
people are in the taxi

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:18.940
or what the current
taxi meter reading is.

00:05:18.940 --> 00:05:22.240
So how do we go about condensing
this data in time and space?

00:05:22.240 --> 00:05:25.720
Well, first we're going to
Window the data in time.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:28.150
What I mean by this is that
we are going to group together

00:05:28.150 --> 00:05:30.704
all points that
occurred sufficiently

00:05:30.704 --> 00:05:31.870
close to each other in time.

00:05:31.870 --> 00:05:34.390
So all points for the
interval one to two--

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:36.980
that's three points here
get grouped together--

00:05:36.980 --> 00:05:38.980
and all the points that
occurred in the interval

00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:40.480
from two to three--
that's one point

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:42.772
here get grouped together.

00:05:42.772 --> 00:05:45.230
The next thing we'd like to do
is group the points together

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:46.160
in space.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:49.280
Again, what we do here
is we assign groupings

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:51.110
based on the location.

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.810
So XY points within a Window
get grouped together--

00:05:53.810 --> 00:05:54.890
that's two points.

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:57.440
KM points within a Window
get grouped together--

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:59.540
that's one point for
the one-to-two Window

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.570
and one point for the
two-to-three Window.

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:06.480
Finally what we
do is we count up

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.250
the points in each such
time and space grouping

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:11.070
and we ready to
emit the results.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:14.640
That's two taxis for our x and
y in the one-to-two interval,

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:16.650
one taxi for km in the
one-to-two interval,

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:19.740
and one taxi for km in
the two-to-three interval.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.530
So what have we actually
accomplished here?

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:25.470
Well we've described a set
of logical transformations

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:28.350
that take us from
our input data--

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.300
our raw taxi
telemetry-- and give us

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.134
something that is much
condensed in terms

00:06:32.134 --> 00:06:34.050
of the volume of the
stream, but still carries

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:37.780
sufficient information to be
correct for our visualization.

00:06:37.780 --> 00:06:40.300
OK, how do we take this
logical description

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:43.240
and actually turn this
into Dataflow code?

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.530
Well, we do it like this.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:49.390
Notice that every line of
code here corresponds exactly

00:06:49.390 --> 00:06:53.320
to a logical transformation
that we described over our data.

00:06:53.320 --> 00:06:55.030
We've encoded the set
of transformations

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.190
that move us from the
raw taxi telemetry

00:06:57.190 --> 00:07:00.640
into a condensed
volume results stream.

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:02.460
Also important, this
is real Dataflow code

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.510
and notably absent from
this code are things

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:07.240
like setting up clusters,
spinning up virtual machines,

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:10.054
deploying resources, recovering
from crashes or faults.

00:07:10.054 --> 00:07:12.470
All of these things are being
handled for you by Dataflow.

00:07:17.263 --> 00:07:19.620
So what are Dataflow programs?

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:22.020
Dataflow programs describe
logical transformations

00:07:22.020 --> 00:07:23.700
over collections of data.

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:26.670
These collections are called
PCollections and maybe bounded

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.530
or unbounded in the
case of data streams.

00:07:29.530 --> 00:07:32.550
The transformations
are called PTransforms,

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:35.610
and they may be executed
over these PCollections

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:37.890
to produce new PCollections.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.630
Together these
allow us to build up

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:42.420
a Pipeline which describes
how our data is transformed

00:07:42.420 --> 00:07:45.600
from the input to
the desired result.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.140
So look at this Pipeline.

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:50.089
We start with a PCollection
which we read in from Pub/Sub.

00:07:50.089 --> 00:07:51.630
And this is an
unbounded PCollection,

00:07:51.630 --> 00:07:55.140
which means that that data is
continuing to arrive forever.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.000
We then apply a
sequence of PTransforms

00:07:57.000 --> 00:08:00.210
to it, creating new collections,
PCollections, at every point.

00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:03.944
And the resulting PCollection
is written back out to Pub/Sub.

00:08:03.944 --> 00:08:06.360
The Pipeline continues to run
forever until it is stopped,

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.174
because this output
PCollection is also unbounded.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:17.780
There we go.

00:08:17.780 --> 00:08:20.600
So we use Transforms such as
Windowing, Mapping, and Count

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:21.350
here.

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:23.540
These are built into
the Dataflow SDK

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:25.160
and you had to do
no additional work

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:27.920
to use these other than just
invoking them in your code.

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:32.600
Other transforms such as
CombinedRides or CondensedRides

00:08:32.600 --> 00:08:33.878
here can be written by you.

00:08:33.878 --> 00:08:36.169
So I'm going to show this
CondensedRide transform here,

00:08:36.169 --> 00:08:39.309
which you wrote and then applied
in parallel over the data,

00:08:39.309 --> 00:08:41.600
which basically what it does
is it just groups together

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:43.945
points that occurred within
100 meters of each other.

00:08:43.945 --> 00:08:46.070
And so this is being run
in parallel over your data

00:08:46.070 --> 00:08:47.236
in a streaming fashion here.

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:54.600
So once we have
written our Pipeline,

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.790
what do we do with it?

00:08:56.790 --> 00:08:59.490
Well we can run it on
Google Cloud Dataflow,

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:03.060
and we tell it some basic things
like where to read the input,

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:05.760
where to write the
output, some parameters

00:09:05.760 --> 00:09:09.270
like the initial number of
workers we want it to use,

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:11.310
and then we run it.

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:13.392
And what's happening
when we actually run it?

00:09:13.392 --> 00:09:14.850
Well lots of things
are being taken

00:09:14.850 --> 00:09:16.860
care of by the back end for us.

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:18.450
It's optimizing
the execution graph

00:09:18.450 --> 00:09:20.340
so that execution
happens more efficiently,

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:23.160
it's spinning up VMs,
it's staging Pipeline code

00:09:23.160 --> 00:09:26.560
to those VMs, it's managing
the I/O with Pub/Sub,

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:29.400
it's managing persistent
state for fault tolerance.

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.500
And by the way, let's talk
about fault tolerance.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.170
If the VM happened to crash
or any other kind of fault

00:09:34.170 --> 00:09:36.540
happens, the Dataflow
execution engine

00:09:36.540 --> 00:09:38.400
will transparently
restart things for you

00:09:38.400 --> 00:09:40.220
and continue to
work, and no data

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:41.970
will be lost because
of the check-pointing

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:45.750
of persistent state about
the Pipeline execution.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.240
So Dataflow
guarantees are always

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:51.120
preserved even in the case of
machine crashes, exceptions,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:54.140
anything else.

00:09:54.140 --> 00:09:57.900
So once this Pipeline is run
here's what it looks like.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.859
This is just a
snapshot of the UI.

00:09:59.859 --> 00:10:01.400
Andrea did a talk
earlier today where

00:10:01.400 --> 00:10:03.804
she went very much
in depth in the UI,

00:10:03.804 --> 00:10:05.970
but this is just a snapshot
of what this looks like.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:08.999
This gives you a little bit more
information about the Pipeline.

00:10:08.999 --> 00:10:10.790
It tells you about the
Pipeline as a whole,

00:10:10.790 --> 00:10:12.539
it tells you about the
steps, it tells you

00:10:12.539 --> 00:10:15.470
how many workers are
processing, how many messages

00:10:15.470 --> 00:10:18.707
are being consumed by
each step in the Pipeline.

00:10:18.707 --> 00:10:20.540
And by the way, if you
notice, this Pipeline

00:10:20.540 --> 00:10:23.370
looks a lot like the logical
diagram that we drew earlier.

00:10:23.370 --> 00:10:26.639
So your Pipeline-centric view
about the transformations

00:10:26.639 --> 00:10:29.180
that you're performing in your
data is maintained in this UI.

00:10:31.915 --> 00:10:33.790
So I want to call your
attention to one thing

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:36.520
here specifically,
which is that if you

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:38.950
can read that over there
the volume of the stream

00:10:38.950 --> 00:10:41.530
fell from about 20,000
messages per second.

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:43.360
After we condensed
in time and space

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:46.220
it fell to about 2000
messages per second--

00:10:46.220 --> 00:10:48.980
a tenfold reduction, which
is the desired outcome.

00:10:48.980 --> 00:10:50.980
So now we can point
our visualizer at this,

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.130
and we see the same results
but now we can keep up.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:59.180
We know we can keep up, because
when we look at the Pub/Sub

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:01.610
subscription statistics
in Stack Driver

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:04.160
we see that our
backlog is not growing.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.110
If you notice there's
an initial spike here.

00:11:06.110 --> 00:11:08.046
This is when the data
flow was spinning up.

00:11:08.046 --> 00:11:10.670
While the workers were coming up
we were building some backlog,

00:11:10.670 --> 00:11:13.190
and then when the worker spun
up and started processing

00:11:13.190 --> 00:11:14.180
they churned
through that backlog

00:11:14.180 --> 00:11:15.888
and then continued to
keep up ever since.

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:25.730
This is one example of a
Dataflow program in action.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:29.000
I want to talk about a
couple of particular aspects

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:33.306
of the Dataflow programming
API that make it interesting.

00:11:33.306 --> 00:11:34.680
Specifically I
want to talk about

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:38.340
how do we deal in Dataflow
with unbounded data.

00:11:38.340 --> 00:11:39.930
Our taxi ride data has no bound.

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:42.660
Events continue to come
in forever, so in general

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.910
as data gets larger we
may want to break it

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:47.850
up into smaller
chunks for processing.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:50.190
If we're using a
traditional batch system

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.290
we might do something
like daily Windows,

00:11:52.290 --> 00:11:53.760
but really this is
just a cheap way

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:57.180
of dealing with data
that is unbounded.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.511
So one of the difficulties
of unbounded data

00:11:59.511 --> 00:12:02.010
is that there may be unknown
delays and data may show up out

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:02.970
of order.

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:04.180
Look at this record here.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:06.630
There's a few records here
that all happened at 8 o'clock.

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:09.690
One occurred and was delivered
to us right away in the stream.

00:12:09.690 --> 00:12:12.460
One occurred and was delivered
to us a little bit later,

00:12:12.460 --> 00:12:14.550
but this green record
that occurred at 8 o'clock

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:17.507
here didn't show up in our
stream until six hours later.

00:12:17.507 --> 00:12:18.840
And who knows why that could be.

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:20.589
That could be from a
multitude of reasons.

00:12:20.589 --> 00:12:22.730
Maybe a user had an event
occur on their phone,

00:12:22.730 --> 00:12:24.480
then they put their
phone in airplane mode

00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:26.589
and didn't turn it
back on for six hours.

00:12:26.589 --> 00:12:28.380
Maybe a server crashed
and didn't come back

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:29.670
online for six hours.

00:12:29.670 --> 00:12:31.560
Maybe there was a
network delay you know,

00:12:31.560 --> 00:12:33.480
an undersea cable
got cut somewhere.

00:12:33.480 --> 00:12:34.170
Who knows.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:36.660
The point is that if we're
writing a stream processing

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.900
system we need to be able
to deal with such delays.

00:12:41.984 --> 00:12:43.400
And depending on
what you're doing

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:45.890
the strategy for dealing with
these delays is also different.

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:48.080
If you're doing some sort of
element wide transformation,

00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:50.090
element-wise transformation
maybe you don't care.

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:51.530
If you're processing
an element at a time

00:12:51.530 --> 00:12:53.654
and you don't care about
when that element occurred

00:12:53.654 --> 00:12:56.090
or the context of that
element, this is probably fine.

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:58.760
But things get a lot
trickier if you're

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:01.880
doing some sort of
grouping or aggregation.

00:13:01.880 --> 00:13:05.370
So one obvious strategy is to
use Processing Time Windows.

00:13:05.370 --> 00:13:07.740
So you slice up your
stream and processing time,

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:09.000
and what do I mean by that?

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.180
Let's say every time
a clock ticks over

00:13:11.180 --> 00:13:13.160
by an hour you draw a
boundary in your stream,

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:14.660
and you process what you have.

00:13:14.660 --> 00:13:16.890
Well this is easy
to understand, sure,

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:19.100
but this probably doesn't
give you what you want.

00:13:19.100 --> 00:13:20.810
If elements are
delayed or out of order

00:13:20.810 --> 00:13:23.309
they're probably not going to
wind up in the same processing

00:13:23.309 --> 00:13:24.680
time buckets.

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:26.450
So for example are
two messages that

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:28.400
both happened at 8 o'clock.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:30.770
If your aggregation involves
grouping them together

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:33.890
or reasoning about them
when they occur together

00:13:33.890 --> 00:13:35.680
you're going to have
a hard time with that

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:37.250
since they're going to be now
in two different processing time

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:37.979
Windows.

00:13:37.979 --> 00:13:40.520
So for example if you're trying
to count taxis that showed up

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:42.320
in the same place
around the same time,

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:46.090
Processing Time Windows are
probably not what you want.

00:13:46.090 --> 00:13:49.370
What we'd really like to have
is an Event Time Windowing

00:13:49.370 --> 00:13:51.230
Strategy.

00:13:51.230 --> 00:13:52.450
As input is arriving.

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:55.070
We want to perform
a Time-Based Shuffle

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:58.010
to place their records into
Windows based on their event

00:13:58.010 --> 00:13:59.510
times.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:03.340
The way to do this in Dataflow
is with the Windowing API.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:04.790
And so Windowing
allows you to do

00:14:04.790 --> 00:14:07.100
is that Windowing allows
you to divide events

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:10.220
into Windows based
on event time so

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:14.780
that you can reason about
them in the correct context.

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:17.180
So Dataflow supports
many kinds of Windows.

00:14:17.180 --> 00:14:20.090
A couple of examples, although
by no means an exhaustive list,

00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:22.580
is something like Fixed
Windows-- the simplest type.

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:25.230
These are Windows that can
be every minute, every hour,

00:14:25.230 --> 00:14:26.750
every day, and so on.

00:14:26.750 --> 00:14:28.490
And these Windows are
typically the same

00:14:28.490 --> 00:14:30.950
across all processing keys.

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:33.902
Another example that's
similar is Sliding Windows.

00:14:33.902 --> 00:14:35.360
These are similar
to Fixed Windows,

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:36.800
but made up of
smaller panes which

00:14:36.800 --> 00:14:39.230
allow them to slide with
time moving forward.

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.480
An interesting thing here
is that the Windows actually

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:43.557
wind up overlapping.

00:14:43.557 --> 00:14:46.140
Another interesting example is
something like Session Windows.

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:50.120
So Sessions are those are
events of activity surrounded

00:14:50.120 --> 00:14:51.950
by periods of inactivity.

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.720
And so an interesting
thing about Sessions

00:14:53.720 --> 00:14:55.520
is that the boundaries
of a Session Window

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:57.940
are functions on
the data itself.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:14:59.600
They're not knowable a priori.

00:14:59.600 --> 00:15:02.180
So they must be computed as
you're processing on your data,

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:05.450
and will depend completely on
the data and very differently

00:15:05.450 --> 00:15:06.500
from key to key.

00:15:06.500 --> 00:15:09.856
So this is not something that
is possible or easy to do

00:15:09.856 --> 00:15:11.480
with a traditional
batch system, but it

00:15:11.480 --> 00:15:13.287
is easy to do with Dataflow.

00:15:13.287 --> 00:15:14.870
Other Windows are,
of course, possible

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:16.490
and are a matter
of implementation

00:15:16.490 --> 00:15:19.380
and whatever the requirements
of your application are.

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:26.390
So Windows let us answer the
question of where in Event Time

00:15:26.390 --> 00:15:29.180
we would like to
perform our aggregation.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:31.370
We still need to answer a
different question which

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:36.020
is, when in processing time we
are ready to emit our results.

00:15:36.020 --> 00:15:37.544
In order to do
that, we first need

00:15:37.544 --> 00:15:39.710
to better define the
relationship between event time

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:41.130
and processing time.

00:15:41.130 --> 00:15:43.040
So let's look at
this graph here.

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:45.559
On the horizontal axis
we have Event Time,

00:15:45.559 --> 00:15:47.600
and on the vertical axis
we have Processing Time.

00:15:47.600 --> 00:15:50.271
And every event that happens
that is delivered to our system

00:15:50.271 --> 00:15:51.770
and processed by
our system is going

00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:54.020
to wind up somewhere
on this graph.

00:15:54.020 --> 00:15:55.910
In an ideal world
where events are

00:15:55.910 --> 00:15:59.210
delivered to us instantaneously
and processed instantaneously,

00:15:59.210 --> 00:16:01.977
every event would line up across
or along this ideal diagonal.

00:16:01.977 --> 00:16:04.310
So it would be delivered and
processed at the same event

00:16:04.310 --> 00:16:07.400
time, as we process the same
Processing Time as the Event

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:08.780
Time of its occurrence.

00:16:08.780 --> 00:16:10.679
Of course reality
is not so nice.

00:16:10.679 --> 00:16:12.470
You know there's network
delays, processing

00:16:12.470 --> 00:16:13.790
is not instantaneous.

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:16.340
So in fact all of our
events wind up somewhere

00:16:16.340 --> 00:16:17.870
above this ideal diagonal.

00:16:21.044 --> 00:16:22.460
Dataflow provides
something called

00:16:22.460 --> 00:16:25.610
the Watermark, which helps
us reason about completeness.

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:28.160
The Watermark tells us how
far above this diagonal

00:16:28.160 --> 00:16:29.870
we can draw a line
beyond which we don't

00:16:29.870 --> 00:16:32.700
expect to see any more events.

00:16:32.700 --> 00:16:36.320
This Watermark is represented
as a red line on this graph.

00:16:36.320 --> 00:16:39.265
If we know everything about
our sources perfectly--

00:16:39.265 --> 00:16:40.640
so if we're reading
from a source

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.850
that we know everything
perfectly and completely about,

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:46.640
the Watermark is a hard
guarantee, that in our system

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:50.245
we're never see anything
beyond this delay.

00:16:50.245 --> 00:16:52.370
Of course in many cases
our knowledge is imperfect,

00:16:52.370 --> 00:16:54.980
and so the Watermark is
also an imperfect guarantee.

00:16:54.980 --> 00:16:57.380
It's really a
heuristic that tells us

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:59.030
to the best of the
system's knowledge

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:03.560
when do we not expect
to see any new data.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:05.060
Either way the
Watermark can tell us

00:17:05.060 --> 00:17:07.495
when we expect to have all
the data for the given Window,

00:17:07.495 --> 00:17:08.869
and therefore when
it is correct,

00:17:08.869 --> 00:17:10.520
as far as we know, to the
best of our knowledge,

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:11.353
to emit the results.

00:17:15.260 --> 00:17:18.010
So if we look again
at the Windowing

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:20.410
as a Time-Based
shuffle, we can see

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:23.470
how the Watermark helps
us know when to emit

00:17:23.470 --> 00:17:25.329
the results of a Window.

00:17:25.329 --> 00:17:27.400
However, the Watermark
may not be everything

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:30.200
that you want to use
for triggering output.

00:17:30.200 --> 00:17:32.620
There are two major
reasons why you might want

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:34.240
to consider something else.

00:17:34.240 --> 00:17:36.940
First of all, the Watermark
may be too slow, so to speak.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:39.670
The Watermark is trying to be
as conservative as possible.

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:40.960
It's trying to keep
track of every event

00:17:40.960 --> 00:17:43.001
in the system, which is
great from the standpoint

00:17:43.001 --> 00:17:45.250
of completeness, but
maybe you don't care.

00:17:45.250 --> 00:17:47.212
If you're doing daily
Windows, for example,

00:17:47.212 --> 00:17:48.670
you're not going
to get your output

00:17:48.670 --> 00:17:50.919
until the end of the day
at the very earliest,

00:17:50.919 --> 00:17:52.960
but you might want some
speculative early results

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:53.630
earlier.

00:17:53.630 --> 00:17:57.130
So the Dataflow triggers
API, allows you to trigger

00:17:57.130 --> 00:17:59.830
early speculative output.

00:17:59.830 --> 00:18:02.571
The Watermark may
also be too fast.

00:18:02.571 --> 00:18:04.570
We already said that if
we don't know everything

00:18:04.570 --> 00:18:06.520
about our sources,
meaning our source

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:08.710
can produce late or
out-of-order data,

00:18:08.710 --> 00:18:10.030
the Watermark may be imperfect.

00:18:10.030 --> 00:18:12.730
And so an event might come
in even after the Watermark

00:18:12.730 --> 00:18:14.500
has already advanced.

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:17.500
In this case Dataflow
provides late data triggers

00:18:17.500 --> 00:18:21.430
that allow you to handle late
data when it occurs in order

00:18:21.430 --> 00:18:23.140
to emit updates to your results.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:30.730
So let's look at building
this example up a little bit.

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:34.390
Here we have Windows
two minute Windows--

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:38.200
Event Time Windows-- that are
being closed by the Watermark.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:39.700
So what we're doing
in these Windows

00:18:39.700 --> 00:18:43.255
is we're building
a running total.

00:18:43.255 --> 00:18:45.370
As processing time
moves forward,

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:47.524
represented as up
in this graph, we

00:18:47.524 --> 00:18:49.690
know when we can close the
Window when the Watermark

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:50.815
passes the Window boundary.

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:55.470
Notice though that
there is one event here

00:18:55.470 --> 00:18:57.060
that's not included in
any Window, that event

00:18:57.060 --> 00:18:59.520
with a value of nine, and
that's because it was late data.

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:02.040
As I described it was an event
that was emitted by a source

00:19:02.040 --> 00:19:03.750
after the Watermark
already advanced

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:06.420
because we didn't know something
perfectly about the source.

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:08.225
So maybe it was
an event that came

00:19:08.225 --> 00:19:09.600
in from somebody's
phone that was

00:19:09.600 --> 00:19:11.100
in airplane mode for six hours.

00:19:11.100 --> 00:19:14.070
And so the late data
trigger API in Dataflow

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:16.650
allows you to handle that and
then emit some sort of updated

00:19:16.650 --> 00:19:17.646
aggregation.

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:26.580
So let's put all of
this together now

00:19:26.580 --> 00:19:30.500
and use it to try and answer
a few questions about our taxi

00:19:30.500 --> 00:19:32.460
data that we saw earlier.

00:19:32.460 --> 00:19:34.790
Let's start with
a simple question.

00:19:34.790 --> 00:19:38.510
At a given point in time how
do the taxi rides from airports

00:19:38.510 --> 00:19:41.842
compare to overall taxi
rides across New York?

00:19:41.842 --> 00:19:43.550
And we're going to
try and use everything

00:19:43.550 --> 00:19:46.704
we learned about Dataflow
to answer that question.

00:19:46.704 --> 00:19:48.620
So the first thing that
we're going to do here

00:19:48.620 --> 00:19:51.780
is we're going to
write a Pipeline.

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:55.370
What it does here is it
filters out the rides that

00:19:55.370 --> 00:19:57.535
originated at airports.

00:19:57.535 --> 00:19:58.410
So how do we do this?

00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:00.130
Well, first of all, we
read things in from Pub/Sub

00:20:00.130 --> 00:20:00.740
as before.

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:08.540
Let's try that again.

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:11.040
The next thing that we do
is we associate the ride ID

00:20:11.040 --> 00:20:13.110
with each ride.

00:20:13.110 --> 00:20:17.494
So the ride ID tells us which
ride this taxi ride point is

00:20:17.494 --> 00:20:19.410
associated with, so that
we can group together

00:20:19.410 --> 00:20:21.618
all the points that are
coming in from a single ride.

00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.160
All right then.

00:20:29.160 --> 00:20:32.310
Next we use Session Windows
to draw Window boundaries

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:33.150
around the ride.

00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:36.409
So we drop Window
boundaries at the beginning

00:20:36.409 --> 00:20:37.950
of the ride, that's
the pickup point,

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:40.351
and 10 minutes after the
drop-off point, which

00:20:40.351 --> 00:20:41.600
is the last point in the ride.

00:20:44.300 --> 00:20:47.600
The next step in this Pipeline
is triggering, and triggering

00:20:47.600 --> 00:20:50.060
controls how results
are delivered

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:51.770
to the subsequent transforms.

00:20:51.770 --> 00:20:54.370
Here we trigger continuously
on every element

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:56.540
so that we ensure that
we emit constant updates

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:57.920
to the visualizer.

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:00.420
We'd like to see the ride's
moving along in the visualizer,

00:21:00.420 --> 00:21:01.790
so we'd like to see updates.

00:21:01.790 --> 00:21:04.940
So we want to make sure
to trigger continuously.

00:21:04.940 --> 00:21:06.990
And here we also use
accumulating triggers

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:08.730
as opposed to
discarding triggers

00:21:08.730 --> 00:21:11.790
to ensure that we receive
full contents of our Window

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:12.970
on every trigger firing.

00:21:15.740 --> 00:21:18.170
We then combine all of
the points in the Window

00:21:18.170 --> 00:21:20.000
on each trigger firing.

00:21:20.000 --> 00:21:22.667
So we only care, in our combiner
we only care about two things.

00:21:22.667 --> 00:21:24.500
We care about the start
point, because we're

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:25.640
going to need the
start point in order

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:28.100
to filter out the rides
that started at an airport.

00:21:28.100 --> 00:21:29.850
And we need the
most recent points

00:21:29.850 --> 00:21:33.360
so that we can emit that
to the visualization.

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:35.930
The AccumulatePoints combine
function implements this

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:37.414
by keeping track
of the start point

00:21:37.414 --> 00:21:39.830
and then updating the most
recent point with the newer one

00:21:39.830 --> 00:21:41.447
if it becomes available.

00:21:44.790 --> 00:21:47.640
Then we filter by discarding
accumulated rides where

00:21:47.640 --> 00:21:49.260
the pickup was
not at an airport,

00:21:49.260 --> 00:21:52.260
and we do this by comparing
the latitude and longitude

00:21:52.260 --> 00:21:56.280
of the pickup with a well-known
set of latitude and longitude

00:21:56.280 --> 00:21:59.180
points for airports JFK,
LaGuardia, and Newark.

00:22:02.910 --> 00:22:04.470
Since when we
actually care about

00:22:04.470 --> 00:22:07.680
is that the output is
only the most recent point

00:22:07.680 --> 00:22:10.050
we use a stage to filter
just the most recent point

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:13.260
from our accumulator.

00:22:13.260 --> 00:22:17.230
Finally we write the
results back out to Pub/Sub.

00:22:17.230 --> 00:22:19.390
So when we run this,
what does that look like?

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:31.499
So we can see that the rides
visible in the visualizer

00:22:31.499 --> 00:22:34.040
are only ones that are starting
at JFK, LaGuardia, and Newark

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:36.380
as desired, and that
the results are still

00:22:36.380 --> 00:22:38.114
being updated in
real-time time which

00:22:38.114 --> 00:22:40.280
is due to our triggering
condition and our carefully

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:41.698
constructed accumulator.

00:22:47.560 --> 00:22:51.130
So the Pipeline we wrote
to filter airport rides

00:22:51.130 --> 00:22:53.502
is only part of our
solution however.

00:22:53.502 --> 00:22:55.810
Dataflow and GCP make
it easy to compose

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:58.332
multiple independent components.

00:22:58.332 --> 00:23:00.290
So far we've talked about
taking the taxi data,

00:23:00.290 --> 00:23:03.760
ingesting it via Pub/Sub, and
pointing our visualizer at it.

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:07.150
However, we want to do
more with this data.

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:10.240
So what we do is we actually put
the results back into Pub/Sub,

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:14.140
and then we read them
through an ETL Pipeline,

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:16.480
that I wrote separately,
that takes those results

00:23:16.480 --> 00:23:19.000
from Pub/Sub and then
writes them into BigQuery.

00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:21.130
This is a very standard
use of Dataflow.

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:23.920
And now we can actually use
that same ETL Pipeline that

00:23:23.920 --> 00:23:26.260
writes the BigQuery and
point it at the raw taxi

00:23:26.260 --> 00:23:30.460
feed to write the raw data
into a separate set of BigQuery

00:23:30.460 --> 00:23:32.980
tables, so we can compare
the BigQuery results

00:23:32.980 --> 00:23:35.240
for the raw data versus
the airport data.

00:23:41.290 --> 00:23:43.730
So what can we learn?

00:23:43.730 --> 00:23:45.310
A simple query
against the raw data

00:23:45.310 --> 00:23:48.130
tells us that in a five-minute
interval of time in New York

00:23:48.130 --> 00:23:51.580
City there is about
1,700 taxi rides that

00:23:51.580 --> 00:23:54.090
drop off somewhere in the
New York greater area.

00:23:54.090 --> 00:23:56.117
And the average cost of
a ride is about 14 bucks

00:23:56.117 --> 00:23:59.200
and the average net revenue
for the taxi company

00:23:59.200 --> 00:24:02.885
is about $25,000 for that
five-minute interval.

00:24:02.885 --> 00:24:05.260
How does that compare, for
the same five minute interval,

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:07.480
how does that compare
with the airport data?

00:24:07.480 --> 00:24:10.540
Well when we point the same
query at the airport table what

00:24:10.540 --> 00:24:12.490
we see is that the
number of rides

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:15.950
is obviously much smaller but
the average-- it's only like 63

00:24:15.950 --> 00:24:18.040
rides I think-- but the
average cost of the ride

00:24:18.040 --> 00:24:20.240
is much, much higher
as you would expect,

00:24:20.240 --> 00:24:23.724
which is about $52 per ride.

00:24:23.724 --> 00:24:26.140
There is also a time-based
component to this data as well.

00:24:26.140 --> 00:24:28.610
If we graphed this
data over time,

00:24:28.610 --> 00:24:31.510
we can see that there is, first
of all, an obvious daily trend

00:24:31.510 --> 00:24:34.484
based on time of day in terms of
the overall rides and the taxi

00:24:34.484 --> 00:24:36.400
rides, but also there's
some interesting event

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:40.270
happening around just before 9
o'clock here in airport rides.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:42.310
Maybe there's a bunch of
big flights that landed

00:24:42.310 --> 00:24:43.340
or something.

00:24:43.340 --> 00:24:46.570
So you can see how you can use
this strategy and these sort

00:24:46.570 --> 00:24:48.820
of tools to dive
deeper into your data.

00:24:51.540 --> 00:24:56.700
So the next thing that
I would like to do

00:24:56.700 --> 00:24:59.100
is I would like to talk
about another cool feature

00:24:59.100 --> 00:25:00.834
of Dataflow service.

00:25:00.834 --> 00:25:02.250
So what we're going
to do is we're

00:25:02.250 --> 00:25:05.470
going to deploy new
code to a running

00:25:05.470 --> 00:25:09.379
Dataflow Pipeline
live, and we're

00:25:09.379 --> 00:25:11.170
going to do this while
keeping all existing

00:25:11.170 --> 00:25:12.753
state and aggregations
for the writing

00:25:12.753 --> 00:25:15.280
Pipeline while adding
new functionality.

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:17.210
So the code that
we're going to update

00:25:17.210 --> 00:25:20.200
is we're going to update
the airport code that I

00:25:20.200 --> 00:25:21.580
was showing you before.

00:25:21.580 --> 00:25:27.190
We're going to update it from
just looking for rides that

00:25:27.190 --> 00:25:29.350
pick up at JFK to
also looking for rides

00:25:29.350 --> 00:25:31.066
that pick up at
LaGuardia and Newark.

00:25:31.066 --> 00:25:33.190
So first of all, let's see
what it looks like right

00:25:33.190 --> 00:25:34.660
now before we update it.

00:25:34.660 --> 00:25:37.150
So before we update
it we see that we

00:25:37.150 --> 00:25:39.160
have a ton of rides
coming out of JFK,

00:25:39.160 --> 00:25:41.510
but not really so much stuff
going on anywhere else.

00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:43.270
There are some rides
scattered elsewhere,

00:25:43.270 --> 00:25:45.795
but really the traffic
is coming from JFK.

00:25:45.795 --> 00:25:47.170
So what we're
going to do here is

00:25:47.170 --> 00:25:50.740
we are going to comment
back in the code that

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:52.870
tracks LaGuardia and Newark.

00:25:52.870 --> 00:25:57.250
We're going to hit
run here, and we're

00:25:57.250 --> 00:26:00.254
going to tell this Pipeline
that it needs to update.

00:26:00.254 --> 00:26:01.420
And that's all I need to do.

00:26:01.420 --> 00:26:04.330
Because the Pipeline
is named it knows

00:26:04.330 --> 00:26:06.400
how to find the old
running Pipeline

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:08.380
and update it with the new code.

00:26:08.380 --> 00:26:10.846
So the update is now
running, and that's

00:26:10.846 --> 00:26:12.220
going to take a
couple of minutes

00:26:12.220 --> 00:26:16.450
and let me actually tab over
to the UI here and show you

00:26:16.450 --> 00:26:20.100
the Pipeline will
eventually here

00:26:20.100 --> 00:26:22.327
go from a state of running
into a state of updating.

00:26:22.327 --> 00:26:24.035
That's going to take
a couple of minutes.

00:26:24.035 --> 00:26:25.951
So let's take a look if
it's already updating.

00:26:34.060 --> 00:26:34.930
Still staging.

00:26:41.420 --> 00:26:44.180
So it's going to start
updating here any second.

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:55.150
While it's starting to update
Rafael is going to talk

00:26:55.150 --> 00:26:56.200
to us about-- oh here it goes.

00:26:56.200 --> 00:26:57.130
So it's starting to update.

00:26:57.130 --> 00:26:59.254
That's going to take just
a couple of minutes here,

00:26:59.254 --> 00:27:01.782
and Rafael is going to tell us
about update in the meantime.

00:27:01.782 --> 00:27:02.990
Back to the presenter please.

00:27:13.350 --> 00:27:15.120
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ:
Can you hear me now?

00:27:15.120 --> 00:27:15.780
Good.

00:27:15.780 --> 00:27:16.620
OK.

00:27:16.620 --> 00:27:19.820
So what Slava has
on right now, he

00:27:19.820 --> 00:27:22.920
has initiated a live
update of the Pipeline

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:25.560
that is computing our time
aggregates of the data stream.

00:27:25.560 --> 00:27:28.034
As you can see he
changed his Java code

00:27:28.034 --> 00:27:29.325
and he's deploying this change.

00:27:29.325 --> 00:27:34.590
So notice how this is one
piece of our entire strategy

00:27:34.590 --> 00:27:37.650
that we've put
together here on GCP.

00:27:37.650 --> 00:27:39.920
There is of course,
a direct approach

00:27:39.920 --> 00:27:42.150
that you could take to
update this piece of code,

00:27:42.150 --> 00:27:46.620
which is bring that Pipeline
down, write a new one,

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:48.480
and resubmit.

00:27:48.480 --> 00:27:52.650
That is probably not
the best we can do.

00:27:52.650 --> 00:27:54.870
In fact Dataflow can do better.

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:57.060
What would happen
in that use case

00:27:57.060 --> 00:27:59.370
if you just simply
bring it down and up,

00:27:59.370 --> 00:28:03.000
first of all, Cloud Pub/Sub
will continue receiving events

00:28:03.000 --> 00:28:06.180
from all the taxis, all those
events are going to accumulate.

00:28:06.180 --> 00:28:09.060
You're going to have some
work to catch up with once

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:12.900
your new Pipeline restarts,
but more importantly, you

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:19.890
will have lost state that was
in-flight while we were running

00:28:19.890 --> 00:28:21.390
the original Pipeline.

00:28:21.390 --> 00:28:23.070
So why would you
even want to do this?

00:28:23.070 --> 00:28:24.570
Why do you want to update?

00:28:24.570 --> 00:28:25.720
There's many reasons.

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:27.870
Adding functionality
is one of them,

00:28:27.870 --> 00:28:30.510
but another very common
scenario and concern

00:28:30.510 --> 00:28:33.570
is what happens when the
libraries that you depend on

00:28:33.570 --> 00:28:37.290
need to be version-ed up
for security reasons, right?

00:28:37.290 --> 00:28:42.090
What happens when you need to
alter parameters or perhaps

00:28:42.090 --> 00:28:44.520
change the type
of machine that's

00:28:44.520 --> 00:28:46.380
backing up your Pipelines etc.

00:28:46.380 --> 00:28:48.190
So these types of
changes are things

00:28:48.190 --> 00:28:53.220
that we want to do with minimal
interruption to the service

00:28:53.220 --> 00:28:55.320
and preserving your
intermediate states.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:29:00.210
So state is really hard here,
and what do I mean by state?

00:29:00.210 --> 00:29:01.826
One of the things
that Dataflow-- hey,

00:29:01.826 --> 00:29:02.820
it started working.

00:29:02.820 --> 00:29:06.990
One of the things that
Dataflow is doing in order

00:29:06.990 --> 00:29:11.250
to guarantee this low
latency delivery of results

00:29:11.250 --> 00:29:14.470
is computing things in
an incremental fashion.

00:29:14.470 --> 00:29:17.970
So if you focus, for example,
in this count accumulator

00:29:17.970 --> 00:29:20.400
near the end, it
really has state

00:29:20.400 --> 00:29:24.150
about the Window and the
group that it's seeing

00:29:24.150 --> 00:29:25.950
and the count so far.

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:30.270
When the Watermark advances
this particular step,

00:29:30.270 --> 00:29:32.250
the system enforces
this particular step

00:29:32.250 --> 00:29:36.210
that it is now correct
to emit a result.

00:29:36.210 --> 00:29:38.880
So the fact that you've been
computing this incrementally

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:40.990
is what allows you to
emit it right away.

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:41.490
OK?

00:29:41.490 --> 00:29:45.330
So you're not going to have to
recompute pieces of the input

00:29:45.330 --> 00:29:46.680
when the time passes.

00:29:46.680 --> 00:29:53.270
So this intermediate state
is really important to us.

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:56.300
What Dataflow is going
to do is it's basically

00:29:56.300 --> 00:29:57.740
going to take care
of that state,

00:29:57.740 --> 00:29:59.420
and move to your new Pipeline.

00:29:59.420 --> 00:30:02.960
This example probably seems
trivial to you, right?

00:30:02.960 --> 00:30:07.190
The Pipeline looks the same, the
state kind of maps one-on-one,

00:30:07.190 --> 00:30:09.860
but there are other things that
the service can do for you too

00:30:09.860 --> 00:30:12.230
because there are
other changes that we

00:30:12.230 --> 00:30:14.690
consider update-compatible.

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:16.580
Examples of
update-compatible changes

00:30:16.580 --> 00:30:19.640
that you may want to
enact on your Pipeline

00:30:19.640 --> 00:30:22.850
include adding and/or
transforming perhaps

00:30:22.850 --> 00:30:26.600
a completely new output path.

00:30:26.600 --> 00:30:31.310
That is perfectly legal, that
is perfectly safe to update.

00:30:31.310 --> 00:30:35.150
Another thing you may want
to do is rearrange the steps

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:36.530
in your Pipeline.

00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:39.770
Perhaps you, because of the
characteristics of your data

00:30:39.770 --> 00:30:41.810
and the characteristics
of your code,

00:30:41.810 --> 00:30:43.970
through experimentation,
you discover

00:30:43.970 --> 00:30:46.040
that rearranging
these steps result

00:30:46.040 --> 00:30:48.800
in a semantically
equivalent Pipeline that

00:30:48.800 --> 00:30:51.320
happens to perform much faster.

00:30:51.320 --> 00:30:53.450
In some cases that occurs.

00:30:53.450 --> 00:30:58.480
The order in which you
apply things matter.

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:01.265
Other things you may want to
do is remove steps, right,

00:31:01.265 --> 00:31:02.640
like the step that
we just added.

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:03.760
We just remove it.

00:31:03.760 --> 00:31:06.040
I put a little star there,
because there is also

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:10.610
a class of updates that
are unsafe to perform.

00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:14.090
These updates usually involve
transforms that are producing

00:31:14.090 --> 00:31:15.770
or consuming side inputs.

00:31:15.770 --> 00:31:19.070
That is, they are away
from the main flow.

00:31:19.070 --> 00:31:22.850
They may be using
keyed state, or they

00:31:22.850 --> 00:31:26.570
may have some operations
that involve Window merging.

00:31:26.570 --> 00:31:27.380
Not to worry.

00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:30.380
If you request such a
change the Dataflow service

00:31:30.380 --> 00:31:34.160
will reject the
update, but it will not

00:31:34.160 --> 00:31:37.080
alter the flow of your
original Pipeline.

00:31:37.080 --> 00:31:41.000
So there are other things that
Dataflow is doing here for you.

00:31:41.000 --> 00:31:45.920
Once a new job that was updated
from a previous job is spun up

00:31:45.920 --> 00:31:48.725
we actually show you the
provenance of which job

00:31:48.725 --> 00:31:50.790
it came from and so on.

00:31:50.790 --> 00:31:53.280
So this is a lot of work that's
being done on your behalf,

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:55.970
so that you don't have to worry
about all these considerations.

00:31:55.970 --> 00:31:58.310
And you can actually
maintain your code even

00:31:58.310 --> 00:32:03.140
when it's powering a
continuous computation.

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:05.310
So Slava, what's going
on with our update?

00:32:05.310 --> 00:32:06.726
SLAVA CHERNYAK:
Let's take a look.

00:32:09.020 --> 00:32:10.920
Demo machine please.

00:32:10.920 --> 00:32:11.420
Great.

00:32:11.420 --> 00:32:14.500
So we can see that the
Pipeline has been updated.

00:32:14.500 --> 00:32:17.810
You can see right here that this
is the same job name running

00:32:17.810 --> 00:32:20.810
and it says that it's updated
from the previous job.

00:32:20.810 --> 00:32:22.370
And when we look
at our visualizer

00:32:22.370 --> 00:32:25.220
now we can see that there
is a bunch of new rides that

00:32:25.220 --> 00:32:26.720
are coming from
LaGuardia now, which

00:32:26.720 --> 00:32:30.680
is what we would expect after
having updated our Pipeline.

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:32.870
A couple of interesting
things to point out

00:32:32.870 --> 00:32:35.720
in this visualization
however, is the fact

00:32:35.720 --> 00:32:39.110
that first of all our rides
from JFK are still all there.

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:41.090
We're not just getting
new rides from JFK,

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:43.400
we still have all of
our old rides from JFK.

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:45.170
This is what Rafael
was talking about.

00:32:45.170 --> 00:32:48.410
We're preserving the
previous Pipeline state.

00:32:48.410 --> 00:32:50.510
Another interesting
thing to point out

00:32:50.510 --> 00:32:54.140
is that we actually also
have new and old rides

00:32:54.140 --> 00:32:56.750
from LaGuardia, not just
new rides from LaGuardia.

00:32:56.750 --> 00:32:59.780
And this is because the
LaGuardia rides were

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:02.551
in the Pipeline
already, we just changed

00:33:02.551 --> 00:33:04.550
the code that was filtering
them out at the end.

00:33:04.550 --> 00:33:07.049
All of that data was already
in the Windows and in the state

00:33:07.049 --> 00:33:08.810
and being accumulated,
we just changed

00:33:08.810 --> 00:33:10.550
what we were emitting at
the end of the Pipeline.

00:33:10.550 --> 00:33:12.140
So by updating our
Pipeline we started

00:33:12.140 --> 00:33:15.200
emitting the full rides that
started at LaGuardia even

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:16.820
before the update ran.

00:33:16.820 --> 00:33:19.190
So this is an interesting
thing about keeping

00:33:19.190 --> 00:33:24.150
the Pipeline state that really
helps you do the right thing.

00:33:24.150 --> 00:33:26.183
In the case of your Pipeline.

00:33:29.890 --> 00:33:33.800
Back to slides please.

00:33:33.800 --> 00:33:35.490
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: Thanks, Slava.

00:33:35.490 --> 00:33:38.130
Not shown here is a
ton of work that's

00:33:38.130 --> 00:33:39.870
happening behind the scenes.

00:33:39.870 --> 00:33:42.150
There are things that
the service, the Dataflow

00:33:42.150 --> 00:33:45.300
can do for you such as
automatically increase

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:48.570
or decrease the size
of the worker pool

00:33:48.570 --> 00:33:52.410
to better cope with incoming
data spikes and lulls,

00:33:52.410 --> 00:33:53.610
because we also scale down.

00:33:53.610 --> 00:33:56.820
We save you money,
that's a good thing.

00:33:56.820 --> 00:34:01.060
Pub/Sub, not shown here,
but this is a Global Publish

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:02.160
Subscribe service.

00:34:02.160 --> 00:34:03.750
It is scalable for you.

00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:07.800
It will give you very
interesting guarantees,

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:11.370
so you can build
this type of system

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:14.460
with correctness and
reliability in mind.

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:17.310
And BigQuery, the
analysts' favorite,

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:19.095
completely managed for you.

00:34:19.095 --> 00:34:22.380
You have no idea how many
machines are doing what or when

00:34:22.380 --> 00:34:23.639
and you shouldn't have to.

00:34:23.639 --> 00:34:25.420
You're just writing queries.

00:34:25.420 --> 00:34:28.620
This is a really powerful
thing to keep in mind

00:34:28.620 --> 00:34:31.230
when you develop on GCP.

00:34:31.230 --> 00:34:34.320
You can use Dataflow
to really think

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:36.600
about the rest of the
services of the platform

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:38.760
as either a source on a sink.

00:34:38.760 --> 00:34:40.780
You can really be
transforming data

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:43.110
as it arrives for
different purposes,

00:34:43.110 --> 00:34:45.719
either for continuous
delivery of results

00:34:45.719 --> 00:34:49.560
or for archival or for further
transformation, and really

00:34:49.560 --> 00:34:51.750
leverage the very
different services

00:34:51.750 --> 00:34:55.199
that we have to enable
the various patterns

00:34:55.199 --> 00:34:58.500
that modern
applications require.

00:34:58.500 --> 00:35:01.650
So in summary, we just
took a closer look

00:35:01.650 --> 00:35:04.020
at some of the technologies
the power of the demos

00:35:04.020 --> 00:35:06.120
that you saw this morning.

00:35:06.120 --> 00:35:09.840
We took a deep dive on how
continuous event processing

00:35:09.840 --> 00:35:13.260
looks like on GCP, in
particular with Dataflow.

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:16.210
You saw a hint of the
programming model.

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:17.790
We think you'll
prefer programming

00:35:17.790 --> 00:35:19.800
at that level instead
of programming

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:22.200
at the machine
level, and we're also

00:35:22.200 --> 00:35:24.780
emphasizing these best
practices of composition

00:35:24.780 --> 00:35:28.385
and really thinking about how
to treat GCP as the platform

00:35:28.385 --> 00:35:30.060
that it is so that
you can light up

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:32.880
different things
in a single flow.

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:35.610
And of course with
streaming update

00:35:35.610 --> 00:35:39.450
you can now take care of really
complicated lively applications

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:42.390
with minimal downtime
and no data loss, which

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:44.310
we think is very important.

00:35:44.310 --> 00:35:47.550
So last I wanted to
talk about Apache Beam.

00:35:47.550 --> 00:35:52.920
So in early 2016 we
announced our intention

00:35:52.920 --> 00:35:57.090
to move the Dataflow programming
model and SDKs, Java,

00:35:57.090 --> 00:36:01.500
and Python we have right now to
the Apache Software Foundation.

00:36:01.500 --> 00:36:05.160
I am very happy to share with
you that Apache Beam is now

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:08.400
a top level project in the
Apache Software Foundation.

00:36:08.400 --> 00:36:11.190
This is again the result
of us paying attention

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:13.170
to developer
productivity at Google,

00:36:13.170 --> 00:36:15.330
developing a new
model that we now

00:36:15.330 --> 00:36:16.860
want to have a
community of people

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:20.700
using in a variety of services.

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:24.000
So what's next for you?

00:36:24.000 --> 00:36:26.700
If you're interested there
are a couple of talks tomorrow

00:36:26.700 --> 00:36:28.170
about Apache Beam.

00:36:28.170 --> 00:36:29.850
The first talk is
really centered

00:36:29.850 --> 00:36:33.420
about the community
aspect and ecosystem

00:36:33.420 --> 00:36:39.380
now that Apache Beam is a top
level project in the Apache

00:36:39.380 --> 00:36:40.710
Software Foundation.

00:36:40.710 --> 00:36:42.810
You will also find
out how we're going

00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:45.720
to move from the
Dataflow SDK to Beam

00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:47.460
and what's the
story around that.

00:36:47.460 --> 00:36:51.720
The second talk goes a little
deeper talks about Portable

00:36:51.720 --> 00:36:54.630
and Parallel Data Processing
using Apache Beam, and what

00:36:54.630 --> 00:36:58.890
this portability means now
that this model is really

00:36:58.890 --> 00:37:00.810
available for the community.

00:37:00.810 --> 00:37:04.110
We have resources for you
as well, a couple URLs there

00:37:04.110 --> 00:37:05.710
for you to find more.

00:37:05.710 --> 00:37:08.700
There's also a code
lab right here,

00:37:08.700 --> 00:37:11.100
if you want to try Dataflow,
you haven't had a chance,

00:37:11.100 --> 00:37:14.740
you can actually write a
program here at the conference.

00:37:14.740 --> 00:37:18.810
There's another code lab that
utilizes this taxi data, which

00:37:18.810 --> 00:37:21.480
is now available to you
as a Public Data Stream,

00:37:21.480 --> 00:37:22.750
so that you can play with it.

00:37:22.750 --> 00:37:26.340
You can learn how to develop
these type of applications,

00:37:26.340 --> 00:37:28.290
and you can access
that code lab at

00:37:28.290 --> 00:37:31.200
Codelabs.developer.google.com.

00:37:31.200 --> 00:37:33.780
So I want to thank you
very much for coming

00:37:33.780 --> 00:37:36.096
and we're happy to
take your questions.

00:37:36.096 --> 00:37:38.088
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:45.580
AUDIENCE: Hi, thank
you for the talk.

00:37:45.580 --> 00:37:50.350
First question is let's say I
had a not a very smart grouping

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:53.020
and I have a lot of
stuff in one group,

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:55.540
and now I run out of
memory in one instance.

00:37:55.540 --> 00:37:57.832
What will happen then?

00:37:57.832 --> 00:37:59.290
SLAVA CHERNYAK: So
there's a couple

00:37:59.290 --> 00:38:01.040
of answers to that question.

00:38:01.040 --> 00:38:03.430
First of all, at the
application level you can--

00:38:03.430 --> 00:38:05.260
so what you're
talking about is also

00:38:05.260 --> 00:38:07.510
called problem of Hot Keys.

00:38:07.510 --> 00:38:09.820
There's a couple of different
ways of handling it.

00:38:09.820 --> 00:38:11.950
One, is at the application
level you can always

00:38:11.950 --> 00:38:15.047
find a better charting strategy,
a better keying strategy

00:38:15.047 --> 00:38:16.630
so that you don't
have those Hot Keys.

00:38:16.630 --> 00:38:18.820
There's also multilevel
combines in Dataflow

00:38:18.820 --> 00:38:21.490
that allow you to essentially
do fan-in to reduce

00:38:21.490 --> 00:38:23.110
the volume to a single key.

00:38:23.110 --> 00:38:27.280
So it is something
that you as a developer

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:28.750
have to be a little
bit aware of.

00:38:28.750 --> 00:38:31.041
It's not something that can
be completely transparently

00:38:31.041 --> 00:38:32.830
handled by Dataflow,
because if you

00:38:32.830 --> 00:38:34.810
throw all of your traffic
against the one key

00:38:34.810 --> 00:38:36.680
at some point there's
nothing we can do.

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:39.832
But as long as you are aware
that that is something that

00:38:39.832 --> 00:38:41.665
is happening there are
tools within Dataflow

00:38:41.665 --> 00:38:43.621
that make it easy to handle it.

00:38:43.621 --> 00:38:46.120
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: So the high
order bid for you to keep here

00:38:46.120 --> 00:38:50.060
is finding a re-keying
strategy even mid-Pipeline

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:52.780
is a very good way to
guard against these very

00:38:52.780 --> 00:38:56.069
Hot Keys or very Hot Groups.

00:38:56.069 --> 00:38:56.610
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:38:56.610 --> 00:38:57.943
And I had a the second question.

00:38:57.943 --> 00:39:00.130
You mentioned basically
these updates that are not

00:39:00.130 --> 00:39:03.580
compatible, with
your current system

00:39:03.580 --> 00:39:05.990
how would you then
run this update?

00:39:05.990 --> 00:39:08.190
So basically, how do I
replace a running system?

00:39:08.190 --> 00:39:09.190
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: Right.

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:10.660
So there are several strategies.

00:39:10.660 --> 00:39:13.760
In some cases what people
do is they suspend flow.

00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:17.470
They know that there
is a computable set

00:39:17.470 --> 00:39:19.810
in the case of temporal
Windows of which Windows

00:39:19.810 --> 00:39:21.250
are going to be
considered "dirty"

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:24.130
because you will
have lost this data.

00:39:24.130 --> 00:39:25.960
So they are prepared
to enact some data

00:39:25.960 --> 00:39:29.250
loss, because the upgrade,
it's the equivalent

00:39:29.250 --> 00:39:31.360
of a breaking change.

00:39:31.360 --> 00:39:33.430
And they are OK in
some circumstances

00:39:33.430 --> 00:39:35.380
to evolve their code that way.

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:38.710
Another strategy that we
have seen used successfully

00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:43.630
is having different pieces of
one single Pipeline actually

00:39:43.630 --> 00:39:48.430
publishing to Pub/Sub using
Pub/Sub as a composition point.

00:39:48.430 --> 00:39:51.880
That allows you to isolate
pieces of the Pipeline

00:39:51.880 --> 00:39:55.030
that you want to
evolve or perhaps

00:39:55.030 --> 00:39:59.260
adding alternative branches
that start to pick up data

00:39:59.260 --> 00:40:00.970
after a particular
point, and then

00:40:00.970 --> 00:40:03.160
shutting down other pieces.

00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:07.450
So that is a strategy to cope
and deal with breaking changes.

00:40:07.637 --> 00:40:08.470
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:40:08.470 --> 00:40:10.690
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: OK.

00:40:10.690 --> 00:40:13.390
AUDIENCE: I have a question
about the lateness.

00:40:13.390 --> 00:40:13.620
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: About what?

00:40:13.620 --> 00:40:14.020
I'm sorry.

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:14.650
AUDIENCE: Lateness.

00:40:14.650 --> 00:40:15.270
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: Lateness.

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:17.290
AUDIENCE: So data that
is out of the Window.

00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:21.820
After the data in the Window
has been gotten and accumulated,

00:40:21.820 --> 00:40:24.176
if something shows
up out of bounds,

00:40:24.176 --> 00:40:25.550
I understand the
default behavior

00:40:25.550 --> 00:40:29.200
is to discard that even with
the lateness parameters set.

00:40:29.200 --> 00:40:29.940
Is that true?

00:40:29.940 --> 00:40:31.148
SLAVA CHERNYAK: Correct, yes.

00:40:31.148 --> 00:40:36.190
AUDIENCE: So in the Beam
SDK how would I actually

00:40:36.190 --> 00:40:37.930
ensure that, first
of all, how would I

00:40:37.930 --> 00:40:40.390
ensure that the data gets
handled and secondly,

00:40:40.390 --> 00:40:42.400
does Beam actually
go and pull down

00:40:42.400 --> 00:40:45.880
the raw data that was used
to do, like, the raw data

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:47.680
from the original Window?

00:40:47.680 --> 00:40:50.710
SLAVA CHERNYAK: So to answer
to your first question,

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:52.810
there is a trigger in
Beam that specifically

00:40:52.810 --> 00:40:55.810
allows you to trigger
a processing based

00:40:55.810 --> 00:40:57.794
on that late data.

00:40:57.794 --> 00:40:59.710
So you do after Watermark
to trigger something

00:40:59.710 --> 00:41:04.060
based on the Watermark and
then you would do after,

00:41:04.060 --> 00:41:07.990
with allowed lateness, to
add the possible lateness

00:41:07.990 --> 00:41:09.794
parameter there.

00:41:09.794 --> 00:41:11.710
I'm not sure I understand
your second question

00:41:11.710 --> 00:41:14.320
about whether it pulls down
the data-- oh you mean,

00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:16.600
does it deliver the
data, the full data

00:41:16.600 --> 00:41:18.580
for the original Window
with your late data?

00:41:18.580 --> 00:41:20.917
AUDIENCE: Well, does it does
it do reprocessing I guess

00:41:20.917 --> 00:41:22.000
is what I'm trying to say.

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:23.170
SLAVA CHERNYAK: So
that's a question

00:41:23.170 --> 00:41:25.295
of whether you use accumulating
or discarding panes

00:41:25.295 --> 00:41:26.449
if I remember correctly.

00:41:26.449 --> 00:41:28.240
So with accumulating
panes every triggering

00:41:28.240 --> 00:41:30.365
is going to deliver the
full contents of the Window

00:41:30.365 --> 00:41:31.650
whether it's on time or late.

00:41:31.650 --> 00:41:33.520
With discarding
panes it's only going

00:41:33.520 --> 00:41:34.990
to deliberate the
delta based off

00:41:34.990 --> 00:41:38.770
of the most recent
triggering execution.

00:41:38.770 --> 00:41:39.354
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:41:39.354 --> 00:41:41.270
SLAVA CHERNYAK: Does
that answer you question?

00:41:41.270 --> 00:41:42.144
AUDIENCE: Not really.

00:41:42.144 --> 00:41:45.737
So the discard actually
just discards all raw data?

00:41:45.737 --> 00:41:47.570
SLAVA CHERNYAK: So
discarding, what it means

00:41:47.570 --> 00:41:50.104
is every time the trigger
fires we deliver the delta.

00:41:50.104 --> 00:41:52.520
We deliver the delta set since
the last triggering firing.

00:41:52.520 --> 00:41:55.220
So if you had two triggers,
one triggering at the Watermark

00:41:55.220 --> 00:41:57.860
and the second triggering off
the late data, with discarding

00:41:57.860 --> 00:42:00.193
panes you would first deliver
the contents of the Window

00:42:00.193 --> 00:42:02.510
at the Watermark, and then
deliver just the late data

00:42:02.510 --> 00:42:04.093
elements since that's
your only delta.

00:42:04.093 --> 00:42:04.957
AUDIENCE: OK

00:42:04.957 --> 00:42:07.040
SLAVA CHERNYAK: With
accumulating panes every time

00:42:07.040 --> 00:42:08.390
the trigger fires
you would deliver

00:42:08.390 --> 00:42:10.450
the entire contents of the
Window, the accumulated

00:42:10.450 --> 00:42:10.949
contents.

00:42:10.949 --> 00:42:13.500
So at the Watermark it
will be whatever is there.

00:42:13.500 --> 00:42:15.810
And at the late
data triggering you

00:42:15.810 --> 00:42:18.080
would deliver the previous
contents plus the late date

00:42:18.080 --> 00:42:18.710
element.

00:42:18.710 --> 00:42:21.160
So whether you use accumulating
or discarding panes

00:42:21.160 --> 00:42:22.910
is a function of what
kind of reprocessing

00:42:22.910 --> 00:42:24.450
you want to do on that data.

00:42:24.450 --> 00:42:26.420
So if you only care
about the late element

00:42:26.420 --> 00:42:27.710
you probably want to
use discarding panes.

00:42:27.710 --> 00:42:29.900
If you want to reprocess the
whole contents of the Window

00:42:29.900 --> 00:42:31.070
you need accumulating panes.

00:42:31.070 --> 00:42:34.010
AUDIENCE: And so is there a
time limit on how far back I

00:42:34.010 --> 00:42:35.860
can set lateness?

00:42:35.860 --> 00:42:39.290
SLAVA CHERNYAK: In practice,
yes, but not at the SDK level.

00:42:39.290 --> 00:42:41.730
So in practice how long
you keep your data around

00:42:41.730 --> 00:42:43.850
is a function of how
much money you're

00:42:43.850 --> 00:42:45.350
willing to pay for
storage, right,

00:42:45.350 --> 00:42:46.820
and how much
latency the Pipeline

00:42:46.820 --> 00:42:49.950
is able to cope with before it's
no longer able to cope with it,

00:42:49.950 --> 00:42:50.450
right?

00:42:50.450 --> 00:42:53.420
So as far as I know,
the SDK doesn't

00:42:53.420 --> 00:42:56.900
enforce any programmatic
limit on it,

00:42:56.900 --> 00:42:59.769
but if you put a year there
and you keep throwing data

00:42:59.769 --> 00:43:01.310
into year-long
Windows, at some point

00:43:01.310 --> 00:43:02.720
things are going
to start to break.

00:43:02.720 --> 00:43:04.970
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: And there
are practical ways for you

00:43:04.970 --> 00:43:07.100
to evaluate and
experiment how much data

00:43:07.100 --> 00:43:10.010
you're willing to hold
on or how much lateness

00:43:10.010 --> 00:43:11.360
you're willing to tolerate.

00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:13.160
The key thing to
pay attention to

00:43:13.160 --> 00:43:15.500
is at what point with the
resources that you have

00:43:15.500 --> 00:43:18.410
provisioned at max
do you actually

00:43:18.410 --> 00:43:21.710
start to back up in
processing, and you are not

00:43:21.710 --> 00:43:24.270
able to keep up anymore.

00:43:24.270 --> 00:43:27.020
So you can establish a
bound, a practically bound

00:43:27.020 --> 00:43:28.910
based on the
characteristics of your data

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:30.920
on your typical computations
by experimentation.

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:32.920
AUDIENCE: Even though the
Window trigger's fired

00:43:32.920 --> 00:43:34.990
processing won't happen until--

00:43:34.990 --> 00:43:36.040
I'll catch up offline.

00:43:36.040 --> 00:43:36.540
I just--

00:43:36.540 --> 00:43:36.750
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: Yeah.

00:43:36.750 --> 00:43:37.250
Happy to.

00:43:37.250 --> 00:43:39.080
Yeah.

00:43:39.080 --> 00:43:40.950
Let's go over here.

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:45.260
AUDIENCE: So I am occasionally
asked to have parameters

00:43:45.260 --> 00:43:47.420
that I can configure
on my Pipeline

00:43:47.420 --> 00:43:49.670
while it's running
without necessarily

00:43:49.670 --> 00:43:51.650
having to do an upgrade.

00:43:51.650 --> 00:43:55.220
Is there a good way to
manage runtime configurable

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:56.360
parameters?

00:43:56.360 --> 00:43:57.770
SLAVA CHERNYAK: There's a
couple of different answers

00:43:57.770 --> 00:43:58.130
to that question.

00:43:58.130 --> 00:44:00.500
So one of the answers is
that your Pipeline is always

00:44:00.500 --> 00:44:03.646
able to read data
from site inputs

00:44:03.646 --> 00:44:05.270
either generated by
the Pipeline itself

00:44:05.270 --> 00:44:06.740
or from some other config store.

00:44:06.740 --> 00:44:09.650
So if you were to do
that within Dataflow land

00:44:09.650 --> 00:44:12.530
you can always
emit a side output

00:44:12.530 --> 00:44:14.930
that then is consumed as
a side input somewhere.

00:44:14.930 --> 00:44:17.270
And that side input was
actually be a small--

00:44:17.270 --> 00:44:22.250
you can think of it as a join of
your data stream with a small,

00:44:22.250 --> 00:44:23.859
not frequently mutated data set.

00:44:23.859 --> 00:44:25.400
So that not frequently
muted data set

00:44:25.400 --> 00:44:27.320
could be your configuration
that if something

00:44:27.320 --> 00:44:29.236
were to update there
then you might change how

00:44:29.236 --> 00:44:30.445
you're doing your processing.

00:44:30.445 --> 00:44:32.569
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: This will
not allow you, however,

00:44:32.569 --> 00:44:34.970
to change things such as
the length of the Windows

00:44:34.970 --> 00:44:36.210
on the fly.

00:44:36.210 --> 00:44:37.820
These are parameters
of your own code.

00:44:37.820 --> 00:44:38.270
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:39.200
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: Yes.

00:44:39.200 --> 00:44:40.449
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:44:40.449 --> 00:44:41.990
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ:
Let's go over here.

00:44:41.990 --> 00:44:44.620
AUDIENCE: Just a quick question.

00:44:44.620 --> 00:44:50.300
For the Watermark, is
it possible to tune in

00:44:50.300 --> 00:44:53.960
or to tweak it?

00:44:53.960 --> 00:44:55.820
Are there any tools around that?

00:44:55.820 --> 00:44:58.850
SLAVA CHERNYAK: So the
answer currently is no.

00:44:58.850 --> 00:45:01.130
So what we do when
we try and establish

00:45:01.130 --> 00:45:03.119
the Watermark is we
basically take everything

00:45:03.119 --> 00:45:04.160
we know about the source.

00:45:04.160 --> 00:45:05.390
We try and model the source.

00:45:05.390 --> 00:45:06.710
We try and keep track
of all the data there,

00:45:06.710 --> 00:45:08.690
and once we get the
data from the source

00:45:08.690 --> 00:45:10.730
we then perfectly track
the rest of the messages

00:45:10.730 --> 00:45:11.940
throughout the system.

00:45:11.940 --> 00:45:16.430
So the goal of the Watermark is
to be as complete as possible

00:45:16.430 --> 00:45:18.650
when tracking the data.

00:45:18.650 --> 00:45:20.980
So there's no tunable
parameters there.

00:45:23.905 --> 00:45:25.780
Can you say more about
what it is that you're

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:26.655
trying to do with it?

00:45:26.655 --> 00:45:28.450
AUDIENCE: So I'm concerned.

00:45:28.450 --> 00:45:31.980
So it may work in
certain applications,

00:45:31.980 --> 00:45:33.330
but it may not work everywhere.

00:45:33.330 --> 00:45:33.830
Right?

00:45:33.830 --> 00:45:36.960
So does it learn from your data?

00:45:36.960 --> 00:45:41.850
Is there a way for it to gather
metrics for a Window of time,

00:45:41.850 --> 00:45:45.349
and then set the parameters
or are those fixed?

00:45:45.349 --> 00:45:47.140
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: So
I'm not sure I follow.

00:45:47.140 --> 00:45:50.220
I think what I hear
you say is two things.

00:45:50.220 --> 00:45:53.350
One is you're talking about
the Windowing strategy

00:45:53.350 --> 00:45:56.360
and the other one is whether you
can control the passage of time

00:45:56.360 --> 00:45:59.240
by basically saying when the
Watermark should advance.

00:45:59.240 --> 00:46:01.421
so if you're interested
in the second one

00:46:01.421 --> 00:46:03.170
there are techniques
and things that we'll

00:46:03.170 --> 00:46:07.505
be happy to talk to you
about that involve triggering

00:46:07.505 --> 00:46:09.500
and different things
that you can do.

00:46:09.500 --> 00:46:12.020
The Watermark, as Slava
said, is a function

00:46:12.020 --> 00:46:15.200
of what the service knows about
the data source, our ability

00:46:15.200 --> 00:46:18.890
to examine, you know, your
allowed lateness that you

00:46:18.890 --> 00:46:21.830
specify plus what we're
seeing in terms of arrival

00:46:21.830 --> 00:46:24.050
and guarantees from
the source, and then we

00:46:24.050 --> 00:46:26.484
know when it is safe to
canonically advance time.

00:46:26.484 --> 00:46:28.400
SLAVA CHERNYAK: And let
me say one more thing.

00:46:28.400 --> 00:46:30.525
Basically, if it is
a custom source that

00:46:30.525 --> 00:46:31.400
you're talking about.

00:46:31.400 --> 00:46:33.950
If it's a source that you wrote
and you know best about then

00:46:33.950 --> 00:46:37.440
you can provide the Watermark
for the service to use.

00:46:37.440 --> 00:46:40.220
So you sort of provide the
Watermark for a custom source

00:46:40.220 --> 00:46:42.740
and then we use that
combined with the Watermarks

00:46:42.740 --> 00:46:44.510
throughout the rest
of the Pipeline.

00:46:44.510 --> 00:46:47.269
So obviously if you know best
about what kind of timestamps

00:46:47.269 --> 00:46:49.310
your service is providing
you are the best source

00:46:49.310 --> 00:46:51.080
for the information on the
Watermark there as well.

00:46:51.080 --> 00:46:52.850
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: One final
strategy that I will mention.

00:46:52.850 --> 00:46:55.010
Again, application
dependent, everything

00:46:55.010 --> 00:46:58.010
we've described deals
with logical time, which

00:46:58.010 --> 00:47:01.940
means every data event
has time and that time

00:47:01.940 --> 00:47:05.600
has meaning and that's
what triggers the Watermark

00:47:05.600 --> 00:47:06.320
advancement.

00:47:06.320 --> 00:47:07.820
There's also real time, right?

00:47:07.820 --> 00:47:13.280
Like where the time advancement
is a function of actual arrival

00:47:13.280 --> 00:47:15.940
so where time is not
part of your data

00:47:15.940 --> 00:47:18.450
but is really a system
function, there are other things

00:47:18.450 --> 00:47:19.860
that you can do there too.

00:47:19.860 --> 00:47:22.670
AUDIENCE: So does the Watermark
take into account both those?

00:47:22.670 --> 00:47:23.461
SLAVA CHERNYAK: No.

00:47:23.461 --> 00:47:26.940
Well so there is the deeper
answer to that question is yes,

00:47:26.940 --> 00:47:28.940
because we actually have
two kinds of Watermark,

00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:30.706
but when we say
Watermarked we really

00:47:30.706 --> 00:47:32.330
talk about big data
watermark, which is

00:47:32.330 --> 00:47:33.789
a Watermark on the event time.

00:47:33.789 --> 00:47:34.330
AUDIENCE: OK.

00:47:34.330 --> 00:47:35.390
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: Yeah.

00:47:35.390 --> 00:47:37.610
AUDIENCE: All right, thank you.

00:47:37.610 --> 00:47:40.220
AUDIENCE: Thanks for
the presentation.

00:47:40.220 --> 00:47:44.210
It was pretty interesting
to see sessions

00:47:44.210 --> 00:47:46.580
as kind of one of
the timing strategies

00:47:46.580 --> 00:47:49.660
so I would like to ask if
sessions in streaming, I mean

00:47:49.660 --> 00:47:51.740
with streaming data, is
possible and if there

00:47:51.740 --> 00:47:55.659
are some efficiency glitches
that we should be cognizant of.

00:47:55.659 --> 00:47:57.200
SLAVA CHERNYAK: So
the first question

00:47:57.200 --> 00:47:59.120
is whether sessions in
streaming is possible.

00:47:59.120 --> 00:47:59.810
Yes, absolutely.

00:47:59.810 --> 00:48:01.700
I mean, it's built
into the Dataflow SDK.

00:48:01.700 --> 00:48:02.760
You can use it right now.

00:48:02.760 --> 00:48:03.020
AUDIENCE: Yep.

00:48:03.020 --> 00:48:03.853
SLAVA CHERNYAK: Yep.

00:48:03.853 --> 00:48:06.820
As far as efficiency
glitches, I mean,

00:48:06.820 --> 00:48:08.650
I think it works as intended.

00:48:08.650 --> 00:48:10.540
I guess I'd have to ask
more about what the--

00:48:10.540 --> 00:48:12.590
AUDIENCE: Event time
versus kind of real time

00:48:12.590 --> 00:48:13.795
kind of slips and stuff.

00:48:13.795 --> 00:48:15.170
I mean when it
comes to sessions,

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:17.570
because it is something
that triggers by itself?

00:48:17.570 --> 00:48:18.487
I mean every message--

00:48:18.487 --> 00:48:20.820
SLAVA CHERNYAK: Well so I
mean, sessions, by definition,

00:48:20.820 --> 00:48:22.490
is going to have
latency in it, right,

00:48:22.490 --> 00:48:24.200
because session
Windows are bounded

00:48:24.200 --> 00:48:25.370
by periods of inactivity.

00:48:25.370 --> 00:48:27.710
So you won't be able to
know that a session has

00:48:27.710 --> 00:48:30.399
ended until you've observed
some time of nothing.

00:48:30.399 --> 00:48:31.940
So there is an
inherent latency built

00:48:31.940 --> 00:48:33.367
into the definition of session.

00:48:33.367 --> 00:48:35.450
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: And the
trade-off for the system

00:48:35.450 --> 00:48:37.010
to be able to
compute sessions is

00:48:37.010 --> 00:48:40.010
going to be space
and resources, right?

00:48:40.010 --> 00:48:42.150
So that's really
what's going on there.

00:48:42.150 --> 00:48:45.200
If you have very choppy data and
a very challenging sessioning

00:48:45.200 --> 00:48:47.750
strategy, likely the
effect is you will

00:48:47.750 --> 00:48:50.690
need more intermediate states.

00:48:50.690 --> 00:48:52.400
AUDIENCE: Which is
the case, apparently.

00:48:52.400 --> 00:48:54.441
I mean, I'm coming from
the advertising industry.

00:48:54.441 --> 00:48:56.330
I mean definitely,
[INAUDIBLE] have

00:48:56.330 --> 00:48:58.910
to kind of side with
people in sessions in order

00:48:58.910 --> 00:49:03.631
to do [INAUDIBLE] on a
micro-scale aggregations.

00:49:03.631 --> 00:49:04.130
OK, thanks.

00:49:04.130 --> 00:49:04.330
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: OK.

00:49:04.330 --> 00:49:04.860
SLAVA CHERNYAK: OK.

00:49:04.860 --> 00:49:05.750
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: All right.

00:49:05.750 --> 00:49:06.916
One more question over here.

00:49:06.916 --> 00:49:09.810
AUDIENCE: My question
is that what languages

00:49:09.810 --> 00:49:11.272
does the SDK currently support?

00:49:11.272 --> 00:49:12.230
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: Sure.

00:49:12.230 --> 00:49:16.970
The SDK is available
in Java and in Python.

00:49:16.970 --> 00:49:19.870
In Python there
are availability--

00:49:22.760 --> 00:49:24.180
Sorry.

00:49:24.180 --> 00:49:27.810
Python supports
batch in Dataflow.

00:49:27.810 --> 00:49:29.700
If you go to the
Apache Beam website

00:49:29.700 --> 00:49:32.700
they have a Python SDK
and a Java SDK already,

00:49:32.700 --> 00:49:35.700
and you'll be able to hear
more about those tomorrow.

00:49:35.700 --> 00:49:38.250
But those are the two languages
that have implemented.

00:49:38.250 --> 00:49:41.520
There are also third
party implementations.

00:49:41.520 --> 00:49:43.980
Part of this community that
you'll hear more about that

00:49:43.980 --> 00:49:47.160
have produced Scala
implementation

00:49:47.160 --> 00:49:48.390
of this called Scio.

00:49:48.390 --> 00:49:50.430
It's built by Spotify.

00:49:50.430 --> 00:49:53.706
And others are starting to be
discussed in the community.

00:49:56.204 --> 00:49:56.870
AUDIENCE: Great.

00:49:56.870 --> 00:49:57.490
RAFAEL FERNANDEZ: OK.

00:49:57.490 --> 00:49:58.406
Well thanks everybody.

00:49:58.406 --> 00:50:00.710
Enjoy your festivities.

00:50:00.710 --> 00:50:03.460
